<body>
<div class='header'>
    <h2>RRB Group of Schools</h2>
    <h3>Pre-Board — Chemistry (Class 12 A)</h3>
</div>
<table width='100%' style='margin-bottom:15px;font-weight:bold;'>
    <tr>
        <td>Time Allowed: 3 Hours</td>
        <td align='right'>Maximum Marks: 70</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
        <td>Teacher: Admin</td>
        <td align='right'>Date: 20/08/2026</td>
    </tr>
</table>
<hr>
<h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section A: Multiple Choice Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Choose the correct answer from the given options.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q1.</strong> What is the van't Hoff factor (i) for a dilute aqueous solution of \ce{K4[Fe(CN)6]} assuming complete dissociation?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 4</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 5</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 2</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q2.</strong> The molar conductivity of \ce{NaCl}, \ce{HCl}, and \ce{CH3COONa} at infinite dilution are 126.4, 425.9, and 91.0 S cm<sup>2</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup> respectively. What is the molar conductivity of \ce{CH3COOH} at infinite dilution?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 516.9 S cm<sup>2</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 390.5 S cm<sup>2</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 201.1 S cm<sup>2</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 390.5 S m<sup>2</sup> mol<sup>-1</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q3.</strong> The SI unit for the rate constant of a second-order reaction is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) s<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) mol L<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) L mol<sup>-1</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) L<sup>2</sup> mol<sup>-2</sup> s<sup>-1</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q4.</strong> Which of the following transition metal ions is paramagnetic with an electronic configuration of [Ar] 3d<sup>3</sup>?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) \ce{Ti<sup>3+</sup>}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) \ce{Cr<sup>3+</sup>}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) \ce{V<sup>3+</sup>}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) \ce{Mn<sup>3+</sup>}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q5.</strong> The IUPAC name of the coordination compound \ce{[Co(NH3)5(CO3)]Cl} is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(III) chloride</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Carbonatopentaamminecobalt(III) chloride</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Pentaamminecarbonatocobalt(II) chloride</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Carbonatopentaamminecobaltate(III) chloride</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q6.</strong> Which of the following alkyl halides undergoes S<sub>N</sub>1 reaction most rapidly?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) \ce{CH3-Cl}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) \ce{CH3-CH2-Cl}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) \ce{(CH3)2CH-Cl}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) \ce{(CH3)3C-Cl}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q7.</strong> When phenol reacts with bromine water, the product obtained is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 2-Bromophenol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 4-Bromophenol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 2,4,6-Tribromophenol</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 2,4-Dibromophenol</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q8.</strong> Which of the following organic compounds will undergo Cannizzaro reaction on treatment with 50% aqueous \ce{NaOH}?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) \ce{CH3CHO}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) \ce{CH3CH2CHO}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) \ce{HCHO}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) \ce{CH3COCH3}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q9.</strong> Hinsberg's reagent, used to distinguish between primary, secondary, and tertiary amines, chemically is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Benzenesulfonyl chloride</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Benzoyl chloride</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Acetyl chloride</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Benzenediazonium chloride</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q10.</strong> Deficiency of which vitamin causes night blindness?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Vitamin B12</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Vitamin C</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Vitamin A</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Vitamin K</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q11.</strong> According to Henry's law, the solubility of a gas in a liquid at a given temperature is directly proportional to its partial pressure. Higher values of Henry's constant (K<sub>H</sub>) indicate:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Higher solubility of the gas</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Lower solubility of the gas</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) No effect on solubility</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Temperature independence</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q12.</strong> How many Faradays of charge are required to reduce 1 mole of \ce{MnO4^-} to \ce{Mn<sup>2+</sup>}?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) 1 F</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) 3 F</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) 5 F</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) 7 F</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q13.</strong> An example of a pseudo-first order reaction is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Decomposition of \ce{H2O2}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Inversion of cane sugar in presence of acid</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Reaction between \ce{NO2} and \ce{CO}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Thermal decomposition of \ce{N2O5}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q14.</strong> Which 3d transition series element shows the highest oxidation state?</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) \ce{Sc}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) \ce{Fe}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) \ce{Mn}</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) \ce{Cr}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q15.</strong> The geometry and hybridization of the complex ion \ce{[Ni(CN)4]^{2-}} are respectively:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Square planar, dsp<sup>2</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Tetrahedral, sp<sup>3</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Square planar, sp<sup>3</sup></li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Octahedral, d<sup>2</sup>sp<sup>3</sup></li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q16.</strong> The reagent used in the Finkelstein reaction for the preparation of alkyl iodides is:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) \ce{NaI} in dry acetone</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) \ce{AgF} in water</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) \ce{PCl5} in ether</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) \ce{ZnCl2} with conc. \ce{HCl}</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q17.</strong> Primary structure of a protein refers to:</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Spatial arrangement of polypeptide chains</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Sequence of amino acids joined by peptide bonds</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) &alpha;-helix and &beta;-pleated sheet structures</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Overall three-dimensional folding pattern</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section B: Assertion-Reason Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Select the correct option from (a), (b), (c), and (d) for each question according to the given statements.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q18.</strong> Assertion (A): Boiling points of alcohols are higher than those of ethers of comparable molecular masses.\nReason (R): Alcohols form extensive intermolecular hydrogen bonding, whereas ethers do not.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q19.</strong> Assertion (A): The complex \ce{[Fe(CN)6]^{3-}} is weakly paramagnetic, while \ce{[Fe(CN)6]^{4-}} is diamagnetic.\nReason (R): \ce{CN^-} is a strong field ligand which causes electron pairing in 3d orbitals.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true.</li></ul><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q20.</strong> Assertion (A): Conductivity of an electrolytic solution decreases with dilution.\nReason (R): The number of ions per unit volume of solution decreases on dilution.</div><ul style='list-style-type:none;padding-left:15px;margin-top:4px;'><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(a) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true and Reason (R) is the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(b) Both Assertion (A) and Reason (R) are true but Reason (R) is NOT the correct explanation of Assertion (A).</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(c) Assertion (A) is true but Reason (R) is false.</li><li style='margin-bottom:2px;'>(d) Assertion (A) is false but Reason (R) is true.</li></ul><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section C: Very Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions briefly.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q21.</strong> State Raoult's law for a solution containing a volatile solute. Define an ideal solution.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q22.</strong> Write the Nernst equation for the following cell reaction at 298 K:\n\ce{Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) -> Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s)}\nCalculate E<sub>cell</sub> if E^&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = 1.10 V, [\ce{Zn<sup>2+</sup>}] = 0.1 M, and [\ce{Cu<sup>2+</sup>}] = 0.01 M.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q23.</strong> Differentiate between the order and molecularity of a chemical reaction with two points of distinction.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q24.</strong> Give reasons for the following:\n(i) Transition elements show variable oxidation states.\n(ii) Transition metals and their compounds generally act as good catalysts.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q25.</strong> Write the IUPAC names of the following coordination entities:\n(i) \ce{K3[Fe(CN)6]}\n(ii) \ce{[Co(NH3)4Cl2]Cl}</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q26.</strong> How will you carry out the following conversions?\n(i) Chlorobenzene to Phenol\n(ii) Phenol to Anisole</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q27.</strong> Give a suitable chemical test to distinguish between Propan-1-ol and Propan-2-ol. Write the chemical equation involved.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q28.</strong> Write the chemical equations involved in the following reactions:\n(i) Stephen reaction\n(ii) Wolff-Kishner reduction</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q29.</strong> Aniline does not undergo Friedel-Crafts reaction (alkylation or acylation). Explain why.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q30.</strong> What are essential and non-essential amino acids? Give one example of each type.</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section D: Short Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following questions in detail.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q31.</strong> A solution prepared by dissolving 15.0 g of a non-volatile non-electrolyte solute in 250 g of water has a freezing point of 272.07 K. Calculate the molar mass of the solute. (Given: Freezing point of pure water = 273.15 K, K<sub>f</sub>  for water = 1.86 K kg mol<sup>-1</sup>).</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q32.</strong> Show that for a first-order chemical reaction, the time required for 99% completion of the reaction is twice the time required for 90% completion.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q33.</strong> Answer the following questions regarding f-block elements:\n(i) What is lanthanoid contraction?\n(ii) State two important consequences of lanthanoid contraction.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q34.</strong> Account for the following observations:\n(i) Haloarenes are extremely less reactive towards nucleophilic substitution reactions compared to haloalkanes.\n(ii) Alkyl halides, though polar, are immiscible with water.\n(iii) Racemic mixture is optically inactive.</div><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q35.</strong> Write chemical equations for the following named reactions:\n(i) Reimer-Tiemann Reaction\n(ii) Kolbe's Reaction\n(iii) Williamson Synthesis</div><h3 style='margin-top:16px;color:#1e1b4b;border-bottom:1px solid #ddd;'>Section E: Long Answer Questions</h3><p style='font-style:italic;color:#555;'>Note: Answer the following long answer questions.</p><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q36.</strong> Answer the sub-questions based on Electrochemistry:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>State Faraday's first law of electrolysis.</li><li>The resistance of a conductivity cell filled with 0.1 M  \ce{KCl} solution is 100\ &Omega;. If the resistance of the same cell when filled with 0.02 M  \ce{KCl} solution is 520\ &Omega;, calculate the conductivity and molar conductivity of the 0.02 M  \ce{KCl} solution. (Conductivity of 0.1 M  \ce{KCl} solution is 1.29 S m<sup>-1</sup>).</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q37.</strong> An organic compound (A) with molecular formula \ce{C8H8O} forms an orange-red precipitate with 2,4-dinitrophenylhydrazine (2,4-DNP) reagent and gives a yellow precipitate of iodoform on heating with iodine in the presence of sodium hydroxide. It neither reduces Tollens' reagent nor Fehling's solution, nor does it decolorize bromine water. On drastic oxidation with chromic acid (\ce{H2CrO4}), it yields a carboxylic acid (B) having molecular formula \ce{C7H6O2}.\n\nBased on the above information, answer the following:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Identify compounds (A) and (B) and write their structural formulas.</li><li>Write chemical equations for the reaction of compound (A) with 2,4-DNP reagent and the iodoform reaction.</li><li>What product is formed when compound (A) is treated with zinc amalgam (\ce{Zn-Hg}) and concentrated hydrochloric acid (\ce{HCl})? Name the reaction involved.</li></ol><div style='margin-bottom:10px;'><strong>Q38.</strong> Answer the sub-questions based on Coordination Chemistry and Biomolecules:</div><ol style='padding-left:20px;margin-top:4px;'><li>Draw the structures of optical isomers of the coordination complex \ce{[Co(en)3]^{3+}}.</li><li>Using Valence Bond Theory (VBT), predict the hybridization, geometry, and magnetic behavior of the complex ion \ce{[Fe(H2O)6]^{3+}}. (Atomic number of Fe = 26).</li><li>What is denaturation of proteins? Explain what happens to the primary, secondary, and tertiary structures of proteins during denaturation.</li></ol>
</body>
</html>